  "query_preview": "..We can't rule out a few daily afternoon \nshowers and storms, but we are in a low grade monsoon pattern with \nthe bigger story shifting back to extreme heat as the mid/upper \nlevel high strengthens and nudges into southeast Arizona. High \npressure aloft is not as conducive to rising motion within the \natmosphere which is needed for storms.\n\nThis afternoon we still have clouds out over central portions of \nPima County which will likely inhibit solar insolation from reaching \nits full potential. Meanwhile, we still do have adequate moisture \naround with surface dewpoints in the upper 50s to low 60s from the \nTucson Metro south and westward. With modest moisture and \ninstability (~1000 J/kg), there is a small chance (10-15%) for a few \nshowers and storms mainly south of Tucson focused mainly over the \nhigh terrain later this afternoon.\n\nTomorrow looks similar to today with the upper high stretching, \nbecoming elongated from southwest to northeast and dragging the \ncenter of the mid level high toward southwest NM/southeast AZ. The \nbest chance (10-15%) for a few showers and storms remains along the \nouter periphery of the high or focused mainly along the \ninternational border from Nogales west.\n\nWednesday into Thursday, the mid/upper high will pretty much be \nstacked overhead which will act to suppress shower and storm \nactivity, but there could be (10-30%) a few storms again on the \nouter periphery of the high pressure where there is less subsidence. \nFriday into Saturday the high shifts east toward New Mexico. \nWhenever a mid/upper high shifts we tend to see stretching of the \nupper levels in the atmosphere helping air be more conducive to \nrising motion. Thus, chances for showers and storms increase again \nareawide on Friday (10-30%) into the weekend (30-50%). That being \nsaid, this is all primarily dependent on the actual evolution of the \nmid/upper level high pressure.\n\nFor now, we have an Extreme Heat Warning in place through 8 PM \nSaturday with high temperatures expected to peak midweek 6 to 10 \ndegrees above normal.",
